# Trivy CVE allowlist for HaraLedger service images.
#
# Each entry below must justify WHY the CVE is not exploitable in our
# pipeline OR runtime, AND name the unblocking action that would let us
# remove it. This file is scanned at every push (.github/workflows/services.yml
# Trivy step) so anything ignored here is being deliberately deferred.
#
# Format: <CVE-ID>  (one per line; # comments allowed)
#
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# node-tar CVEs (bundled inside Node 22 LTS's npm + pnpm 10.x)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# All six CVEs below are in node-tar v7.5.2 (or 6.x), the version shipped
# inside Node 22's bundled npm and inside pnpm 10.26.1's install bundle.
# Fixed in node-tar 7.5.3 / 7.5.4 / 7.5.7 / 7.5.8 / 7.5.10 / 7.5.11 — none
# of which have shipped to Node LTS or pnpm release yet.
#
# Exploit mechanism: malicious tarball passed to `npm install` /
# `pnpm install` triggers path-traversal / symlink-poisoning during
# extract.
#
# Why we're not exploitable:
#   1. Build-time: pnpm install runs against a committed lockfile
#      (services/pnpm-lock.yaml). No arbitrary tarball resolution.
#   2. Runtime: production containers run `pnpm start` only — never
#      `pnpm install`. There is no tar-extract path in the runtime
#      surface area.
#
# Unblock action: when Node 22 LTS or pnpm 10.x ships an update bundling
# node-tar >= 7.5.11, rebuild + re-scan; this allowlist becomes a no-op.
# Track upstream: https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/main/CHANGELOG_V22.md
#                https://github.com/pnpm/pnpm/releases
#
# Filed 2026-05-22 alongside the node:20-alpine → node:22-bookworm-slim
# base-image migration. Remove these lines when the upstream patches land.
CVE-2026-23745
CVE-2026-23950
CVE-2026-24842
CVE-2026-26960
CVE-2026-29786
CVE-2026-31802

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# pnpm CVEs
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Our Dockerfiles pin pnpm via `corepack prepare pnpm@10.26.1 --activate`
# AFTER `rm -rf /usr/local/lib/node_modules/pnpm`. Despite this, `pnpm
# --version` inside the built image still reports 9.12.0 — corepack
# manages its own internal cache (`~/.cache/node/corepack`) which carries
# the Node-22 default pnpm 9.12.0, and Trivy scans that cache too.
#
# Real fix: multi-stage build with a distroless runtime that has neither
# pnpm nor npm. Filed as a separate task ("Multi-stage Dockerfile +
# distroless runtime") which is the canonical attack-surface reduction.
#
# Why these are not exploitable in our pipeline today:
#   CVE-2025-69262 — pnpm tokenHelper env-var leak. We do not configure
#                    or use the tokenHelper feature anywhere.
#   CVE-2025-69263 — pnpm lockfile integrity bypass. We use
#                    `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile=false` AGAINST a
#                    committed services/pnpm-lock.yaml. No attacker can
#                    inject an alternative lockfile at install time, and
#                    our runtime never re-installs.
#
# Filed 2026-05-22. Remove when distroless multi-stage builds land.
CVE-2025-69262
CVE-2025-69263
